DESCRIPTION:
  Config-file-gated command injection. The server must be started with
  --config pointing to a file containing exec_mode=1 and exec_logging=1.
  The cmd parameter is wrapped in a sh -c shell invocation (I2). A strict
  metachar sanitizer (S3) is present but only activates with --strict-exec;
  omitting that flag leaves the endpoint fully exploitable.

CONFIG_GATE (C2 -- config file):
  mechanism:  Key=value config file parsed by read_exec_config() via
              fopen/fgets/sscanf into struct exec_cfg {enabled, logging}.
              CLI only accepts --config PATH (and --strict-exec for S3).
  check:      `if ( exec_cfg.enabled && exec_cfg.logging && strncmp(path, "/exec", 5) == 0 ... )`
  complexity: requires modelling fopen->fgets->sscanf data flow into struct fields

INPUT_FLOW (I2 -- buffered):
  entry:      HTTP GET /exec?cmd=<payload>  ->  global `query`
  path:       handle_exec() extracts cmd = query + 4
              snprintf(shell_cmd, sizeof(shell_cmd), "sh -c '%s' 2>/dev/null", cmd)
              system(shell_cmd)
  transforms: one snprintf buffer wrapping in sh -c

SANITIZATION (S3 -- config-gated metachar check):
  mechanism:  static int strict_exec set by --strict-exec CLI flag;
              if strict_exec: strpbrk(cmd, "|;&`$") -> reject 403
  bypass:     omit --strict-exec; strict_exec stays 0, check never runs

SINK:        system(shell_cmd) in handle_exec() (mini_httpd.c, injected before do_file)

